<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning from Mistakes</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn from This Experience?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Develop arguments based on one or more texts studied. Reading: Identify the main ideas of persuasive texts and their arguments. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can develop arguments based on reading texts about personal growth (e.g., "Why is it good to learn from mistakes?"). Can identify the main ideas of persuasive texts about learning strategies (e.g., "What is one way to improve your grades?"). Can demonstrate phonemic awareness by recognizing and correctly pronouncing minimal pair sounds (e.g., /ð/ in "they" and /d/ in "day") to enhance pronunciation accuracy during reading activities.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays an image of a student looking frustrated with a low grade on a test, then another image of the same student looking happy after studying and getting a better grade. The teacher asks, "What happened in the first picture? What about the second? What did the student learn?" Students share brief ideas, activating prior knowledge related to `mistakes`, `grades`, and `improvement`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes sentences on the board: "I studied hard, and my grades *improved*." and "I *practiced* reading *carefully*." The teacher reads them aloud, exaggerating the /d/ sound in "improved" and /t/ in "practiced", and clearly articulating "carefully" and also contrasting the minimal pair /ð/ in "they" with /d/ in "day" using example sentences like "They had a great day." The teacher demonstrates how to read with attention to these sounds to aid comprehension later.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it good to make mistakes?" (Expected answer: "No, but we can learn.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we change our `study habits` to get `better grades`?" (Expected answer: "Yes, we can improve.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short persuasive blog post titled "Turning Mistakes into Milestones" about a student who faced academic challenges and `improved` their `grades` by changing their `study habits` and seeking `feedback`. The text includes target vocabulary (`mistakes`, `grades`, `study`, `habits`, `homework`, `feedback`, `progress`, `improvement`, `success`, `better`, `successful`, `challenging`, `carefully`, `well`) and grammatical structures (Past Simple, Future tense, "should").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Identify the main ideas of persuasive texts and their arguments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text quickly and answer: "What is the main message of the blog post?" (Expected answer: We can `learn` from `mistakes` to achieve `success`.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the text more `carefully` and decide if statements related to the text are True or False (e.g., "The student never `improved` their `grades`." - False; "Asking for `feedback` helped `progress`." - True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match paragraphs with main sub-headings (e.g., "Recognizing the Problem," "Taking Action," "Seeing `Improvement`").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of texts containing Past Simple, Future tense, "should" for advice, target vocabulary (`mistakes`, `grades`, `study`, `habits`, `homework`, `feedback`, `progress`, `improvement`, `success`, `better`, `successful`, `challenging`, `carefully`, `well`), and recognition of minimal pair sounds (`they`/`day`, `think`/`sink`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Cloze Activity):</b> Students receive a printed version of the blog post with 5-7 target vocabulary words removed. They work individually to fill in the blanks using a word bank that includes words like `mistakes`, `grades`, `study`, `improvement`, `feedback`, `success`, `practiced`. The teacher circulates, providing support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Rebuilding):</b> The teacher provides sentences from the text, jumbled or with missing parts, focusing on `Past Simple` experiences and `Future tense` `improvements`. E.g., "I / learned / study / harder / `will`." (I learned, so I `will` `study` harder.) or "She / `should` / ask / questions / more." (She `should` ask more questions.). Students rewrite or complete the sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Reading & Phonemic Awareness Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a list of sentences containing minimal pairs relevant to the reading (e.g., "They had a busy `day`," "Do you `think` about the `sink`?"). Students take turns reading these sentences aloud to their partner, focusing on differentiating sounds like /ð/ vs /d/ and /θ/ vs /s/. The partner listens `carefully` and provides constructive `feedback` on pronunciation accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning from Mistakes")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Argument Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. They re-read the blog post "Turning Mistakes into Milestones" and discuss its content. Their task is to formulate one strong argument based on the text to answer the question: "Why is it important to learn from our `mistakes` to achieve academic `success`?" They must use at least three vocabulary words from the list (`study`, `improve`, `feedback`, `progress`, `habits`) and at least one "should" statement for advice in their argument. Each group then prepares to present their argument to another group or the class, taking turns to speak.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b> The teacher monitors group discussions, offering guidance on vocabulary and grammar use. Reminds students to refer to ideas from the text and to use "I learned..." (Past Simple) and "We `should`..." (advice) effectively. Encourages them to `try` their best to express their ideas `well`.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> As groups present their arguments, the teacher uses an observation checklist. Other students listen and use a simple peer feedback form to note down one thing they liked about the argument and one piece of advice ("You `should`...") for improvement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of the text's main ideas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Develops a clear argument related to `learning from mistakes`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (`mistakes`, `grades`, `improvement`, `should`, `study`, `success`) `well`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces key words (including minimal pairs if applicable to the argument) intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates effectively within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One main idea I learned from the text today is: `I learned`..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One way I `will study` `better` next time is: `I will`..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What `challenging` parts did you find in reading the text and forming your argument? How did you overcome them?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one piece of advice (`You should...`) you would give to someone who makes a `mistake` and wants to `improve` their `grades`?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked `well` in helping students identify main ideas and arguments? The use of the persuasive blog post and structured discussion seemed effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed? Some students still struggle with distinguishing minimal pairs during rapid speech. More targeted pronunciation `practice` in `challenging` words might be needed for specific sounds. `Feedback` on sentence structure for arguments could be more detailed next time.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3, students `will practice` speaking and interacting more directly using the vocabulary and grammatical structures from this reading. They `will` share their own experiences of `learning from mistakes` and giving `advice` (`should`) to classmates.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>